is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: mswitch0
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Layer‑3 routing on the Campus‑1 L3 switch (mswitch0) — specifically its upstream routing/FRR daemon. Mechanism: Campus‑1 PCs send traffic for Campus‑2 (10.5.0.0/24, 10.6.0.0/24) to their default gateway 172.16.x.254 on mswitch0. Because mswitch0 has no route to those networks, it immediately returns ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable,” so hosts report Campus‑2 as unreachable while intra‑Campus‑1 inter‑VLAN traffic still works.

Localization: On mswitch0, ip route shows only connected routes (172.16.10/20/30 and 10.2.2.0) with no default or learned routes; pinging 10.5.0.1 from mswitch0 fails with “Network is unreachable.” From a Campus‑1 PC (e.g., pc5), ping to 10.5.0.1 returns “From 172.16.10.254 Destination Net Unreachable,” and traceroute stops at 172.16.10.254 with “!N,” confirming the gateway is the rejecting hop. The uplink to router16 is healthy (mswitch0↔10.2.2.1 ping OK), and router16 can reach 10.5.0.1 directly, proving the rest of the path is fine. On mswitch0, FRR is inactive (systemctl shows frr.service inactive; FRR running config empty), so no dynamic routing/default is present.

Root cause: FRRouting on mswitch0 is down/missing configuration, leaving mswitch0 without a default or routes to Campus‑2.

Corrective fix: Restore routing on mswitch0. Start/enable FRR and configure OSPF with router16 to advertise 172.16.10/20/30 and learn upstream networks, or add a static default route via 10.2.2.1 (e.g., “ip route add default via 10.2.2.1”) to restore inter‑campus reachability. Ensure FRR is enabled at boot. 
=== END ===